{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1409 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "7194f6f96d44b757cd7d877f312faaecb7ba8a8bbcdbc13872f988f974297462" :edb/db-before-t 1408 :edb/db-after-t 1409 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534721 50 #inst "2026-10-08T05:05:34.782Z" 13194139534721 true] [17592186105511 1145 "Rearranging the dot product formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= \\frac{a \\cdot b}{\\mid a \\mid \\mid b \\mid} \\\\\n&= \\frac{a_{x}b_{x} + a_{y}b_{y} + a_{z}b_{z}}{\\sqrt{a_{x}^{2} + a_{y}^{2} + a_{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{b_{x}^{2} + b_{y}^{2} + b_{z}^{2}}} \\\\\n&= (2 \\cdot 1 + (-1) \\cdot 5 + 1 \\cdot (-2))/(\\sqrt{2^{2} + (-1)^{2} + 1^{2}}\\sqrt{1^{2} + 5^{2} + (-2)^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-5}{\\sqrt{6} \\cdot \\sqrt{30}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-5}{\\sqrt{6} \\cdot \\sqrt{6} \\cdot \\sqrt{5}} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos \\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}}{6} \\right) \\approx 111.9^\\circ\n$$" 13194139534721 false] [17592186105511 1145 "Rearranging the dot product formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 5+1\\cdot (-2)}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{30}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos \\left( -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}}{6} \\right) \\approx 111.9^\\circ.\n$$" 13194139534721 true] [17592186105512 1100 "Find $\\angle BAC$ given the points $A(2,-1,-5)$, $B(-1,0,-4)$, and $C(5,1,1)$. Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." 13194139534721 false] [17592186105512 1100 "Find $\\angle BAC$ given the points $A(2,-1,-5)$, $B(-1,0,-4)$, and $C(5,1,1).$ Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." 13194139534721 true] [17592186105512 1145 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨2,-1,-5⟩,\\,b=⟨-1,0,-4⟩,\\,c=⟨5,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $\\triangle ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $\\angle BAC = \\theta.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040103/assets/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-4⟩-⟨2,-1,-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,1,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨5,1,1⟩-⟨2,-1,-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,2,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 6}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{11}\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{7\\sqrt{11}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{7\\sqrt{11}} \\right) \\approx 92.5^\\circ.\n$$" 13194139534721 true] [17592186105512 1145 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\na = ⟨2,-1,-5⟩, b = ⟨ - 1, 0,-4⟩, c = ⟨5, 1, 1⟩\n$$\n\nThe triangle $\\triangle ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote\n\n$$\n\\angle BAC = \\theta\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1278/Source/Images/3326328c02627ffa40dbcdf7cb16cc96.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB^(→) &= b - a \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 1, 0,-4⟩ - ⟨2,-1,-5⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 3, 1, 1⟩ \\\\\nAC^(→) &= c - a \\\\\n&= ⟨5, 1, 1⟩ - ⟨2,-1,-5⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨3, 2, 6⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= (AB^(→) \\cdot AC^(→))/(\\mid AB^(→) \\mid \\mid AC^(→) \\mid) \\\\\n&= (-3 \\cdot 3 + 1 \\cdot 2 + 1 \\cdot 6)/(\\sqrt{(-3)^{2} + 1^{2} + 1^{2}}\\sqrt{3^{2} + 2^{2} + 6^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{11}\\sqrt{49}} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{7\\sqrt{11}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{7\\sqrt{11}} \\right) \\approx 92.5^\\circ\n$$" 13194139534721 false] [17592186105529 1145 "First, since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}=-4\n$$\n\n, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na \\cdot b &= -4 \\\\\nt \\cdot 6 + (-2) \\cdot t &= -4 \\\\\n6t - 2t &= -4 \\\\\n4t &= -4 \\\\\nt &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\na = ⟨ - 1,-2⟩, b = ⟨6,-1⟩\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= \\frac{a \\cdot b}{\\mid a \\mid \\mid b \\mid} \\\\\n&= \\frac{a_{x}b_{x} + a_{y}b_{y}}{\\sqrt{a_{x}^{2} + a_{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{b_{x}^{2} + b_{y}^{2}}} \\\\\n&= (-4)/(\\sqrt{(-1)^{2} + (-2)^{2}} \\cdot \\sqrt{6^{2} + (-1)^{2}}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5} \\cdot \\sqrt{37}} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{185}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos \\left(-\\dfrac{4}{\\sqrt{185}} \\right) \\approx 107.1^\\circ\n$$" 13194139534721 false] [17592186105529 1145 "First, since $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}=-4$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =-4 \\\\ t\\cdot 6+(-2)\\cdot t & =-4 \\\\ 6t-2t & =-4 \\\\ 4t & =-4 \\\\ t & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨-1,-2⟩,\\,b=⟨6,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{6}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{37}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{185}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos \\left( -\\dfrac{4}{\\sqrt{185}} \\right) \\approx 107.1^\\circ.\n$$" 13194139534721 true] [17592186105654 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305566 13194139534721 true] [17592186105654 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305580 13194139534721 true] [17592186105654 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305601 13194139534721 true] [17592186105655 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305587 13194139534721 true] [17592186105677 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305573 13194139534721 true] [17592186105677 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305594 13194139534721 true] [17592186105677 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305608 13194139534721 true] [17592186106031 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534721 true] [17592186106031 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nFind the angle between $a = ⟨3, 2, 1⟩$ and $b = ⟨1, 2, 3⟩$. Round your answer to the nearest degree." 13194139534721 false] [17592186106031 1100 "Find the angle between $a=⟨3,2,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,2,3⟩.$ Round your answer to the nearest degree." 13194139534721 true] [17592186106031 1145 "Rearranging the dot product formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 3}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{14}\\cdot \\sqrt{14}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{14} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{5}{7})≈{44}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139534721 true] [17592186106032 1145 "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨0,4,5⟩,\\,q=⟨1,5,4⟩,\\,r=⟨2,3,6⟩.\\end{ali