{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)={90}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139534721 false] [17592186106033 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534721 true] [17592186106033 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nFind $∠BAC$ given the points $A(3,-3, 1)$, $B(5,-3, 3)$, and $C(3,-2, 3)$. Round your answer to the nearest degree." 13194139534721 false] [17592186106033 1100 "Find $∠BAC$ given the points $A(3,-3,1)$, $B(5,-3,3)$, and $C(3,-2,3).$ Round your answer to the nearest degree." 13194139534721 true] [17592186106033 1145 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨3,-3,1⟩,\\,b=⟨5,-3,3⟩,\\,c=⟨3,-2,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040103/assets/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨5,-3,3⟩-⟨3,-3,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,0,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,3⟩-⟨3,-3,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{8}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{